# Citation metadata, in the Citation File Format. GitHub reads this and renders
# a "Cite this repository" button with APA and BibTeX.
#
# There is no DOI yet. A DOI needs an archived release, which needs the Zenodo
# integration switched on for this repository and a tagged release cut. Until
# that exists, this file deliberately says nothing about a DOI rather than
# citing one that does not resolve.
#
# When citing a specific measure, cite the package id and version from its
# openquality.yaml, not just this repository. A measure named in a methods
# section has to stay resolvable to the exact logic that produced the number.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Open Quality: an open corpus of healthcare quality measures and their implementation knowledge"
message: "If you use this corpus in research, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Eugene
    family-names: Vestel
    email: eugene.vestel@gmail.com
repository-code: "https://github.com/FHIR-IQ/openquality"
url: "https://openquality.us"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - clinical quality measures
  - eCQM
  - CQL
  - SQL-on-FHIR
  - FHIR
  - QI-Core
  - health informatics
  - open data
abstract: >-
  An open corpus of healthcare quality measures paired with a typed record of
  what implementers have learned about each one. Measure packages carry
  machine-readable provenance pinned to an upstream commit and re-verified in
  continuous integration, so a redistribution claim is checked rather than
  asserted. Alongside the measures sits a knowledge corpus of interpretation
  issues, defects, implementation notes, and test cases, keyed to a measure and
  pinned to a version, so that questions about how a measure behaves are
  queryable rather than rediscovered.
